According to the discussion in Section 4 , the partial information decomposition discloses that the primary difference between the unlearned model and the original model is that the unlearned model has removed the memorization information associated with the unlearning dataset. Thus, it is essential to verify the presence of memorization information to validate the unlearning results. However, we find that existing metrics are insufficient for evaluating memorization in unlearning ( Thudi et al., 2022 ; Zhang et al., 2024 ) . In order to address this gap, we propose a novel unlearning metric specifically designed to evaluate memorization. Direct evaluation of the memorization information F m F_{m} is challenging. Therefore, we move to the example level and indirectly assess whether the examples with memorization information have indeed been forgotten, to validate the unlearning process. Specifically, we develop an evaluation based on the memorization scores ( Feldman, 2020 ) . Grouped Memorization Evaluation (GME) aims to describe the performance of subgroups categorized by varying memorization scores within the unlearning dataset. GME consists of two steps: 1) Calculating unlearning memorization scores independently and grouping examples based on the scores; 2) Evaluating the performance of each subgroup on the unlearned model. The Unlearning Memorization Score of each example ( x i , y i ) (x_{i},y_{i}) in the unlearning dataset D u D_{u} can be determined using: (11) mem ( f , D r , D u , ( x i , y i ) ) = Pr Φ G ← f ⁡ ( D r ∪ D u ) [ Φ G ( x i ) = y i ] − 1 J ∑ j = 1 J Pr Φ G r j ← f ⁡ ( D r ) [ Φ G r j ( x i ) = y i ] , \displaystyle	ext{mem}(f,D_{r},D_{u},(x_{i},y_{i}))=\Pr_{\Phi_{G}\leftarrow f(D_{r}\cup D_{u})}[\Phi_{G}(x_{i})=y_{i}]- rac{1}{J}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\Pr_{\Phi_{G_{r}}^{j}\leftarrow f(D_{r})}[\Phi_{G_{r}}^{j}(x_{i})=y_{i}], where D u D_{u} refers to the unlearning dataset, and D r D_{r} denotes the remaining dataset. To mitigate randomness, J J retrain